    "body": "## Purpose\n\nWith `activation_scheme=\"dynamic\"`, FP8 linear layers pick per-token activation scales only when `cutlass_fp8_supported()` is true. That helper is CUDA-only, so XPU always falls back to **per-tensor** dynamic quantization. Per-tensor dynamic quant needs a separate global max-reduction pass before quantizing, which is noticeably slower on XPU.\n\n`XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel` already lists `kFp8DynamicTokenSym` as supported, and `torch.ops._xpu_C.fp8_gemm` already accepts `[M, 1]` activation scales, so only the selection logic needs to change.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `dynamic_per_token_fp8_supported()` in `w8a8_utils.py`, returning `current_platform.is_xpu() or cutlass_fp8_supported()`. `cutlass_fp8_supported()` keeps its CUTLASS-only meaning.\n- Use it in `Fp8LinearMethod` and `Fp8PerTensorOnlineLinearMethod`.\n\nNo behavior change on CUDA, ROCm, or CPU.\n\nNote: on XPU this affects the W8A8 path (`XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel`, e.g. `--kernel-config '{\"linear_backend\": \"xpu\"}'`). With `linear_backend=auto`, XPU currently selects the weight-only `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel`, where activations are not quantized.\n\n## Test Plan\n\nIntel Arc Pro B70, single GPU, Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, `quantization=\"fp8\"` (online), `linear_backend=\"xpu\"`:\n\n- Check the selected activation quant key and the scale shape passed to `fp8_gemm`\n- Greedy generation, baseline vs patched\n- Op-level timing of dynamic quant + `fp8_gemm`, per-tensor vs per-token\n\n## Test Result\n\n**Selection**: baseline `QuantKey(f8e4m3fn, scale(f32, dynamic, per_tensor))` -> patched `QuantKey(f8e4m3fn, scale(f32, dynamic, per_token))`.\n\n**Generation**: 3 of 4 greedy prompts produce identical output. The fourth diverges after about 40 tokens with an equivalent continuation (normal numerical drift).\n\n**Op-level (ms, dynamic quant + fp8_gemm, bf16 input, per-tensor FP8 weight)**:\n\n| m, n, k | quant T -> K | gemm T -> K | total T -> K | speedup |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4096, 256, 8192 | 0.729 -> 0.215 | 0.233 -> 0.237 | 0.736 -> 0.486 | 1.51x |\n| 4096, 1024, 8192 | 0.463 -> 0.217 | 0.908 -> 0.921 | 1.461 -> 1.233 | 1.18x |\n| 4096, 2048, 8192 | 0.467 -> 0.221 | 1.857 -> 2.057 | 2.604 -> 2.362 | 1.10x |\n| 4096, 4096, 4096 | 0.295 -> 0.137 | 1.935 -> 2.091 | 2.429 -> 2.283 | 1.06x |\n| 8192, 8192, 8192 | 0.977 -> 0.450 | 17.204 -> 17.357 | 18.195 -> 17.651 | 1.03x |\n| 32, 4096, 4096 | 0.053 -> 0.030 | 0.054 -> 0.054 | 0.091 -> 0.068 | 1.34x |\n| 512, 4096, 4096 | 0.053 -> 0.030 | 0.229 -> 0.229 | 0.271 -> 0.263 | 1.03x |\n\nRelative error vs fp32 reference is the same for both (about 0.037).\n\n`ruff check` / `ruff format --check` pass.\n",
